# Code Quality

## Style

- Prefer simple, robust JavaScript — no build step for runtime code
- async/await for asynchronous operations
- JSDoc on public methods where helpful
- Use `this.log.*` — never `console.log` in production code
- Tabs, double quotes (see `prettier.config.mjs`)
- ESLint via `@iobroker/eslint-config` (`eslint.config.mjs`) — run `npm run lint` after every change

## Resource cleanup

Use adapter-core interval helpers; clear in `onUnload()` and always call `callback()`:

```javascript
onUnload(callback) {
	try {
		if (this.syncInterval) {
			this.clearInterval(this.syncInterval);
			this.syncInterval = null;
		}
		if (this.wallboxClient) {
			this.wallboxClient.close();
			this.wallboxClient = null;
		}
		callback();
	} catch (error) {
		this.log.error(`Error during unload: ${error.message}`);
		callback();
	}
}
```

## Error handling

- Wrap async/API work in try/catch; log errors, do not crash the adapter
- Use adapter log levels: error, warn, info, debug
- Handle network failures gracefully; set `info.connection` accordingly
- Do not retry failed Influx queries in a tight loop — wait for the next poll cycle

## HTTP

- **Preferred:** native `node:http` / `node:https` (used in `queryInflux()`)
- **Avoid:** axios, request, or other HTTP libraries

## Dependencies

- Node.js >= 22
- Use `@iobroker/adapter-core`; avoid unnecessary new dependencies
- Prefer built-in Node.js modules where possible (e.g. `WebSocket` in Node 22)
- Small, focused changes over large refactorings without need

## Linting

After every relevant code change:

1. `npm run lint` (or `npm run lint-fix` first, then fix remaining issues manually)
2. `npm run check` when types or `tsconfig.check.json` are affected
